[% PageVersion = 'Documentation/changelog/2026-08-11-deploy-pull-before-recreate.tt,v 1.01 2026/08/11 shanta Exp' %]
[% IF c.session.debug_mode == 1 %][% PageVersion %][% END %]

[% META title = 'Production never received a push — build: in prod compose made up rebuild instead of pull' %]
[% META description = 'Root cause of "Pull & Deploy never updates production1": docker-compose.prod.yml web-prod had BOTH build: and image:, so docker compose up --force-recreate rebuilt from the host git context and ignored the pulled registry image, pinning prod to a stale 8/4 build. Removed build: (pull-only) and added explicit docker pull + --no-build to deploy.sh.' %]
[% META author = 'shanta' %]
[% META date = '2026-08-11' %]
[% META roles = 'admin developer' %]

<div class="changelog-entry">
    <header class="changelog-header">
        <h1>Production never received a push (build: made <code>up</code> rebuild, not pull)</h1>
        <div class="changelog-meta">
            <span class="date">August 11, 2026</span>
            <span class="author">by shanta</span>
            <span class="type">Bug Fix — Critical deploy path</span>
        </div>
    </header>

    <section class="changelog-summary">
        <p>
            After every Push + Pull &amp; Deploy, prod's banner stayed on
            <code>Git: main@c58120ed+local</code> / Build <code>2026-08-01</code> /
            image <code>9c7bb1e9d715</code> (8/4). CSS and all other fixes never reached
            production1. The push itself was fine — the registry <code>:latest</code> carried
            the new build. The deploy simply never ran it.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="changelog-details">
        <h2>Root cause (proven)</h2>
        <p>
            <code>docker-compose.prod.yml</code> (the file <code>deploy.sh</code> resolves
            <code>COMPOSE_FILE</code> to on prod, line 95) defined <code>web-prod</code> with
            <strong>both</strong> a <code>build:</code> block <em>and</em>
            <code>image: shantamcsbain/comserv-web-prod:latest</code>. When a compose service
            carries <code>build:</code>, <code>docker compose up -d --force-recreate</code>
            <strong>rebuilds from the local build context and ignores the pulled registry
            image</strong>. Prod's build context was its stale git checkout, so every deploy
            re-baked the 8/4-era code and re-tagged it <code>:latest</code> — the pulled push
            was discarded on the very next <code>up</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Proven with an isolated repro: a <code>build:</code>+<code>image:</code> service,
            <code>docker pull</code> the new image, then <code>up --force-recreate</code>
            <strong>rebuilds</strong> (old behavior). Adding <code>--no-build</code> makes
            <code>up</code> run the <em>pulled</em> registry image
            (<code>df71dc1fa4bf</code> = the 9-min-old Hub build) — confirmed identical
            image IDs. The registry side was never the problem:
            <code>docker pull shantamcsbain/comserv-web-prod:latest</code> returns
            <code>sha256:90561894…</code> (built minutes earlier), "Image is up to date."
        </p>

        <h2>Fix</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><code>docker-compose.prod.yml</code> — removed the <code>build:</code> block
                from <code>web-prod</code>. It is now pull-only (matches the already-correct
                <code>script/docker-compose.server.yml</code>).</li>
            <li><code>deploy.sh</code> (main deploy, ~1494) — replaced
                <code>docker compose pull web-prod</code> with an explicit
                <code>docker pull "$IMAGE"</code> that <strong>aborts if the pull fails</strong>
                (no silent fallback to a stale local image), and added
                <code>--no-build</code> to the <code>up --force-recreate</code>.</li>
            <li><code>deploy.sh</code> (monitor self-heal, ~883) — same: explicit
                <code>docker pull "$IMAGE"</code> + <code>--no-build</code> on both recreate
                branches.</li>
            <li>Rollback path (1159) deliberately left as a local-image recreate — pulling
                there would defeat rollback to a known-good backup.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Verified</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><code>bash -n script/deploy.sh</code> — clean.</li>
            <li>No <code>build:</code> key remains under <code>web-prod</code> in
                <code>docker-compose.prod.yml</code> (only the explanatory comment).</li>
            <li>Isolated repro: <code>up --force-recreate --no-build</code> runs the pulled
                registry image, not a local rebuild (image IDs match).</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Action to land the current build</h2>
        <p>
            Re-run <strong>Pull &amp; Deploy</strong> on production1. With the fix, prod pulls
            the freshly-pushed <code>:latest</code> (e.g. <code>sha256:90561894…</code>) and
            recreates with <code>--no-build</code>, so the container will now report the new
            commit/build and CSS + all pending fixes reach production deterministically.
        </p>

        <h2>Files changed</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><code>docker-compose.prod.yml</code> — <code>web-prod</code> now pull-only</li>
            <li><code>script/deploy.sh</code> — explicit pull + <code>--no-build</code></li>
            <li><code>root/Documentation/changelog/2026-08-11-deploy-pull-before-recreate.tt</code> — this entry</li>
            <li><code>root/Documentation/changelog/CHANGELOG.tt</code> — [Unreleased] bullet</li>
        </ul>
    </section>
</div>
